550:{"timestamp":"2026-07-22T04:24:21.599Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_OyueOKbEeSzqgE9EnCD1LJtN","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\npython3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy\npython3 -m py_compile solution.py test_solution.py\npython3 test_solution.py\n\nkompile reference-semantics/semantics.k \\\n  --backend llvm \\\n  --main-module MPY-KRUN \\\n  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\n  --output-definition runtime-kompiled \\\n  --warnings none\nkrun solution.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled\npython3 py2mpy.py smoke.py > smoke.mpy\nkrun smoke.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled\n\nkompile verification.k \\\n  --backend haskell \\\n  --main-module VERIFICATION \\\n  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\n  --output-definition verification-kompiled \\\n  --warnings none\n\n# Prove the loop theorem without assumptions.\nkprove spec.k \\\n  --definition verification-kompiled \\\n  --spec-module SPEC \\\n  --claims SPEC.loop-invariant \\\n  --warnings none\n\n# Use exactly that proved theorem as a lemma while proving the entry claim.\nkprove spec.k \\\n  --definition verification-kompiled \\\n  --spec-module SPEC \\\n  --trusted SPEC.loop-invariant \\\n  --warnings none\n\nif kprove spec-vacuity.k \\\n     --definition verification-kompiled \\\n     --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY \\\n     --warnings none; then\n  echo \"ERROR: false-result mutation unexpectedly proved\"\n  exit 1\nelse\n  echo \"EXPECTED FAILURE: false-result mutation rejected\"\nfi\n\nif kprove spec-body-mutation.k \\\n     --definition verification-kompiled \\\n     --spec-module SPEC-BODY-MUTATION \\\n     --warnings none; then\n  echo \"ERROR: body mutation unexpectedly proved\"\n  exit 1\nelse\n  echo \"EXPECTED FAILURE: body mutation rejected\"\nfi\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f87fd-3122-7f02-98e5-268b319a54ac"}}}
577:{"timestamp":"2026-07-22T04:26:25.060Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_08d8a1371b6b257a016a60463ddd1881969608fc0472b8c049","status":"completed","call_id":"call_NrbLwAxkVYsoEAP4eWwxatKa","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /work/PROOF.md\\n+VALIDATED\\n+\\n+## What is proven\\n+\\n+Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, for every finite `IntList` `XS`, loading\\n+the exact translated `sum_product` definition and calling it on the embedded\\n+Python list `list(intVals(XS))` reaches\\n+\\n+```k\\n+tuple(vCons(sumFrom(0, XS),\\n+            vCons(productFrom(1, XS), .ValSeq)))\\n+```\\n+\\n+with the caller environment restored, an empty call stack, `noRet`, `NoExc`,\\n+exit code 0, and unchanged heap/heap counter.  This is a partial-correctness\\n+reachability theorem.  The final module-scope map is deliberately existential:\\n+it is not observable in the HumanEval return-value contract.\\n+\\n+The formal input domain is exactly finite lists of arbitrary-precision\\n+integers.  `intVals(.IntList) = .ValSeq` and\\n+`intVals(iList(I, R)) = vCons(I, intVals(R))`, so every such `IntList` denotes\\n+the corresponding reference-semantics list.  Non-integer elements are outside\\n+the stated `List[int]` domain.\\n+\\n+The two result functions are left folds matching the program's assignments:\\n+\\n+```text\\n+sumFrom(a, [])       = a\\n+sumFrom(a, i :: xs)  = sumFrom(a + i, xs)\\n+productFrom(a, [])      = a\\n+productFrom(a, i :: xs) = productFrom(a * i, xs)\\n+```\\n+\\n+Consequently the empty input yields `(0, 1)`, and nonempty inputs yield the\\n+sum and product of all elements in iteration order.\\n+\\n+## Proof structure\\n+\\n+`SPEC.loop-invariant` starts at the reference semantics' actual `#loop` head.\\n+With current accumulators `SUM` and `PRODUCT` and unprocessed input `XS`, it\\n+reaches `(sumFrom(SUM, XS), productFrom(PRODUCT, XS))`.  The empty case uses\\n+the fold bases.  The cons case executes the real target binding and both real\\n+integer assignments, then reapplies the circularity to the structurally\\n+smaller tail.  It continues through the exact return and the supplied `#pop`.\\n+\\n+`SPEC.sum-product` starts at `#loadAll` with the exact module AST from\\n+`solution.mpy`, resolves `sum_product` through the module scope, creates the\\n+real call frame, binds `numbers`, executes the three initial assignments, and\\n+instantiates the loop theorem with seeds 0 and 1.\\n+\\n+The installed prover does not automatically use a separately proved claim as\\n+a lemma.  The suite therefore first proves `SPEC.loop-invariant` without\\n+assumptions, then runs the whole spec with exactly that label passed through\\n+`--trusted`.  In the second command the local trust is discharged by the\\n+first command's independent `#Top` result.\\n+\\n+## Proof-extension inventory\\n+\\n+| Extension | Class and domain | Semantic role and state footprint | Justification and dependents |\\n+|---|---|---|---|\\n+| `IntList`, `intVals` and its two equations | Definitional representation; all `IntList` constructors | Names the canonical `ValSeq`; no configuration cells | Equations are disjoint, exhaustive, and structurally decreasing. Used by both claims. |\\n+| Two `#iterNext(list(intVals(...)))` rules | Operational bridge; empty and cons `IntList` cases | Replaces one `intVals` equation followed by the matching supplied list-iterator rule; rewrites only `<k>` | Empty produces `#iterDone`; cons produces exactly `#iterYield(I, list(intVals(R)))`. Cases are disjoint and exhaustive. Used by the loop claim. |\\n+| Exact `For(Name(\\\"number\\\"), Name(\\\"numbers\\\"), BODY)` rule | Operational bridge; exact plain local frame and embedded integer-list value | Reads `<env>`/`<scopes>`, changes only `<k>` to the supplied `#loop` form | Composition of the pure local lookup of `numbers` and `MPY-CONTROLS`' `For` rule. It pins the selected binding, exact body, and parent frame. Used by both claims. |\\n+| Exact `Return(TupleExpr(total, product))` rule | Operational bridge; exact plain local frame with integer accumulators and `noRet` | Reads `<env>`/`<scopes>`, writes `<k>` and `<ret>`; the supplied `#pop` still restores env, scopes, stack, and scope counter | Composition of two pure local lookups, tuple construction, and `MPY-FUNCTIONS`' return rule. Used by both claims. |\\n+| `sumFrom`, `productFrom` equations | Definitional summaries; all accumulators and `IntList` values | Constrain the result; do not replace program execution and touch no cells | Each pair is disjoint, exhaustive, total, and structurally decreasing. Used by both claims. |\\n+| `SPEC.loop-invariant` | Derived auxiliary reachability theorem | Summarizes actual loop execution, return, and frame pop | Proved independently with `#Top`; admitted only as the named lemma for the entry proof. |\\n+| Existential final `<scopes>` | Explicitly framed, unobserved state | Does not constrain the final module map | HumanEval observes the returned tuple. Env, stack, ret, exception, exit, heap, and counters remain constrained. |\\n+\\n+The `For` and `Return` bridges address Haskell-backend cooling failures for\\n+symbolic list/tuple values.  They do not bypass the loop body, integer\\n+arithmetic, call setup, or frame cleanup.\\n+\\n+## Exact commands and actual results\\n+\\n+The complete reproducible runner is `./prove.sh`; its final run exited 0.\\n+These are the substantive commands it ran:\\n+\\n+```bash\\n+python3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy\\n+python3 -m py_compile solution.py test_solution.py\\n+python3 test_solution.py\\n+\\n+kompile reference-semantics/semantics.k \\\\\\n+  --backend llvm --main-module MPY-KRUN --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\\\\n+  --output-definition runtime-kompiled --warnings none\\n+krun solution.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled\\n+python3 py2mpy.py smoke.py > smoke.mpy\\n+krun smoke.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled\\n+\\n+kompile verification.k \\\\\\n+  --backend haskell --main-module VERIFICATION \\\\\\n+  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\\\\n+  --output-definition verification-kompiled --warnings none\\n+\\n+kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC \\\\\\n+  --claims SPEC.loop-invariant --warnings none\\n+kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC \\\\\\n+  --trusted SPEC.loop-invariant --warnings none\\n+\\n+kprove spec-vacuity.k --definition verification-kompiled \\\\\\n+  --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY --warnings none\\n+kprove spec-body-mutation.k --definition verification-kompiled \\\\\\n+  --spec-module SPEC-BODY-MUTATION --warnings none\\n+```\\n+\\n+Actual results:\\n+\\n+- Translation, Python compilation, both `kompile` commands, and both `krun`\\n+  commands exited 0.  Each `krun` result had `<k> .K </k>`, `NoExc`, and\\n+  `<exit-code> 0 </exit-code>`.  `smoke.mpy` executed the prompt examples plus\\n+  negative/zero cases.\\n+- `python3 test_solution.py` exited 0 and printed\\n+  `checked=19613 mismatches=0`.\\n+- The untrusted focused invariant proof exited 0 and printed `#Top`.\\n+- The whole-spec entry proof with only `SPEC.loop-invariant` admitted exited 0\\n+  and printed `#Top`.\\n+- The false-result mutation exited 1 with `WarnStuckClaimState`; its residual\\n+  result was `tuple(vCons(0, vCons(1, .ValSeq)))`, while the mutation required\\n+  `(1, 1)`.  `prove.sh` printed\\n+  `EXPECTED FAILURE: false-result mutation rejected`.\\n+- The product-seed body mutation exited 1 with `WarnStuckClaimState`; its\\n+  residual result was `tuple(vCons(2, vCons(0, .ValSeq)))`, while the original\\n+  postcondition required `(2, 2)`.  `prove.sh` printed\\n+  `EXPECTED FAILURE: body mutation rejected`.\\n+\\n+The protected inputs remained unchanged: the observed SHA-256 values were\\n+`84dc98e731928675a91c68cbff1f89d0677596f849fa0e6b34fa9b40335fce03`\\n+for `prompt.py` and\\n+`406485eac118431c08ef59f30f79dfa6aa9d5825408917fc5ce7c806e664db16`\\n+for `py2mpy.py`, matching `run-input.json`.\\n+\\n+## Gate A — real-program soundness: PASS\\n+\\n+- A1: the entry claim loads the exact function body and uses ordinary lookup,\\n+  call creation, parameter binding, assignments, loop arithmetic, and pop.\\n+  The deliberate product-seed body mutation is rejected with the changed\\n+  result `(2, 0)`.\\n+- A2/A3: every bridge's complete footprint is recorded above.  The bridges\\n+  pin the current local frame, exact bindings, exact source AST, evaluation\\n+  order, return control, and the unchanged cells.  Valid `List[int]` inputs\\n+  have no modeled exceptional branch in these operations.\\n+- A4: all definitional equations have disjoint constructor cases, complete\\n+  coverage, and structural descent.  The iterator bridge cases agree exactly\\n+  with the representation and fixed iterator equations.\\n+- A5: `XS = .IntList` is a satisfiable witness.  The entry postcondition\\n+  constrains both tuple components, and the off-by-one mutation is rejected at\\n+  the concrete residual `(0, 1)`.\\n+\\n+## Gate B — intent adequacy: PASS\\n+\\n+- The formal domain is exactly the prompt's finite `List[int]` domain; no\\n+  numeric range restriction is added.  K `Int` and Python `int` are both\\n+  arbitrary precision for the modeled operations.\\n+- List iteration order, integer addition/multiplication, tuple construction,\\n+  and the empty identities 0 and 1 agree with the prompt and CPython behavior\\n+  on the stated domain.  The unused typing import is a semantics no-op and\\n+  does not affect the function result.\\n+- The summary definitions are the direct left-fold meaning of sum and product.\\n+  Both prompt examples and negative/zero cases agree.\\n+- The intentionally existential final scope map is outside the HumanEval\\n+  observable; the return value and normal completion are constrained.\\n+\\n+## Gate C — trust and evidence auditability: PASS\\n+\\n+Trust ledger:\\n+\\n+- The supplied `MPY` semantics, K v7.1.293 implementation, and SMT/backend are\\n+  the base trusted computing environment for all claims.\\n+- `SPEC.loop-invariant` is locally trusted only in the entry-proof invocation;\\n+  its identical claim is independently proved without trust immediately\\n+  beforehand.  `SPEC.sum-product` is its only dependent.\\n+- The proof-local bridge equivalences depend on the explicitly cited supplied\\n+  rules and exact pattern guards.  Their concrete end-to-end behavior is also\\n+  exercised by the LLVM smoke artifact; finite tests are evidence, not their\\n+  universal justification.\\n+\\n+Reproducible evidence:\\n+\\n+- `smoke.py`/`smoke.mpy`: exact same function AST as `solution.py`; inputs\\n+  `[]`, `[1,2,3,4]`, `[-2,3,0]`, and `[-2,-3]`; assertions are the oracle;\\n+  LLVM `krun` exited 0 with no exception.\\n+- `test_solution.py`: independent oracle `(sum(xs), math.prod(xs))`; the five\\n+  explicit boundary/example cases plus every list of lengths 0 through 5 over\\n+  values `-3..3`; 19,613 checks and zero mismatches.\\n+- `spec-vacuity.k` and `spec-body-mutation.k`: exact commands, witnesses,\\n+  residuals, and nonzero exits are recorded above.\\n+\\n+## Excluded behavior\\n+\\n+- Inputs containing non-integers, infinite iterables, concurrent list\\n+  mutation, Python resource exhaustion, and behavior outside the supplied\\n+  semantics subset are not claimed.\\n+- The theorem does not constrain the final module-scope map because it is not\\n+  part of the requested function result.\\n+- Finite testing is not treated as a universal proof.  Termination is not a\\n+  separate liveness theorem; the reported K result is the kit's\\n+  partial-correctness reachability proof.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f87fd-3122-7f02-98e5-268b319a54ac"}}}
