+
+## 4. Adequacy and real-program pinning
+
+### Formal claims in plain language
+
+`SPEC.sum-product` has no explicit `requires` clause. Its syntactic input `XS:IntList` restricts it to every finite empty/cons list of K integers. Starting in the supplied initial configuration, it loads a `Module`, calls the module binding `sum_product` on `list(intVals(XS))`, and requires the final `<k>` result to be exactly:
+
+```k
+tuple(vCons(sumFrom(0, XS),
+            vCons(productFrom(1, XS), .ValSeq)))
+```
+
+It also requires caller environment 0, `scopeLoc` 1, unchanged empty heap and heap counter, empty stack, `noRet`, `NoExc`, and exit code 0. Only the final scope map is existential. That map contains function/module bindings outside the HumanEval return-value observable; it does not make either tuple component free.
+
+`SPEC.loop-invariant` starts at the supplied semantics' real `#loop` control point. Its pattern requires a plain local frame with current accumulators `SUM` and `PRODUCT`, remaining list `XS`, loop target `number`, a return continuation, and a real call frame. There is no additional logical `requires`. It says execution of all remaining iterations, the translated `Return`, and `#pop` produces the tuple `(sumFrom(SUM,XS), productFrom(PRODUCT,XS))`, restores the caller, removes the frame, and preserves heap/exception/exit state.
+
+### Exact program identity
+
+The entry claim spells associative list units explicitly (`.Stmts` and `.Exprs`), whereas program syntax omits them. A raw whitespace-only comparison therefore differs and is preserved as [04-program-pinning-raw-diagnostic.log](evidence/04-program-pinning-raw-diagnostic.log). The reviewer extractor [program_pinning.py](evidence/program_pinning.py) removed only those associative units and produced byte-normalized equality ([04-program-pinning-extract.log](evidence/04-program-pinning-extract.log)). More importantly, fresh `kast` parsing of the submitted module and the extracted entry module produced identical KAST files with SHA-256 `e9ccd06ad22fefe1c05a78b794c242feacb20bc15fc1135e45f80ba751e86768`; see [04-kast-program-pinning.log](evidence/04-kast-program-pinning.log). The `<k>` cell therefore loads the actual submitted translated program, not a substitute.
+
+The loop claim's body is the exact `For` body from that parsed module. The entry path uses ordinary supplied rules for module sequencing, definition binding, callee lookup, call-frame allocation, parameter binding, all three initial assignments, both loop-body assignments, integer addition/multiplication, and frame pop. The only accelerated control points are audited in Stage 5.
+
+### Satisfiable witnesses and concrete substitution
+
+An entry witness is `XS = .IntList` in the literal supplied initial configuration. It denotes the real call `sum_product([])` and reduces the claimed result to `(0,1)`.
+
+A realizable loop witness is:
+
+```text
+XS=.IntList, INPUT=.IntList, SUM=0, PRODUCT=1, LAST=0,
+LOCAL=1, CALLER=0, NEXT=2, CONT=.K,
+STORE=.Map, HEAP=.Map, HEAPLOC=0, RESTSTACK=.List
+```
+
+This is the loop-head state after the actual empty-input call has initialized its locals. It satisfies the loop pattern and returns `(0,1)` while popping the frame.
+
+For `XS = iList(1,iList(2,iList(3,iList(4,.IntList))))`, the formal RHS unfolds to `(10,24)`. Additional substitutions for `[]`, `[-2,3,0]`, and `[10^30,-2]` all matched both Python implementations; exact encodings and values are in [04-claim-substitution.log](evidence/04-claim-substitution.log), generated by [claim_substitution.py](evidence/claim_substitution.py).
+
+The returned value is an exact two-element tuple, not an unconstrained variable, tautology, or one-way implication. Stage 4 passes.
+
+## 5. Rule-by-rule static soundness review
+
+### Exhaustive inventory
+
+The reviewer inventory includes the supplied root/helper semantics, `verification.k`, and `spec.k`. It records source file, line, declaration kind, all relevant attributes, and normalized full declaration text for every item. The complete inventory is [k-rule-inventory.tsv](evidence/k-rule-inventory.tsv), generated by [k_rule_inventory.py](evidence/k_rule_inventory.py); generation command/hash are in [05-generate-full-inventory.log](evidence/05-generate-full-inventory.log).
+
+Totals are:
+
+- 943 inventoried items: 230 syntax declarations, 705 ordinary rules, 5 contexts, 1 configuration, and 2 reachability claims;
+- 146 function-bearing declarations, 110 with `total`, 0 `functional`, and 0 simplification rules;
+- 33 priority rules, 33 concrete rules, 28 `owise` rules, and 25 opaque `symbol(...)` declarations;
+- `verification.k`: 3 syntax declarations and 10 rules;
+- `spec.k`: 2 claims.
+
+Per-file counts are in [05-inventory-summary.log](evidence/05-inventory-summary.log), attribute counts in [05-attribute-summary.log](evidence/05-attribute-summary.log), and all attribute-bearing source locations in [05-opaque-and-attributes.log](evidence/05-opaque-and-attributes.log).
+
+Every inventory row has a reviewer decision and rationale in [k-rule-assessment.tsv](evidence/k-rule-assessment.tsv), generated by [k_rule_assessment.py](evidence/k_rule_assessment.py). The 943 decisions comprise 70 supplied items checked on the executed path, 13 proof-local items checked below, 2 adequate claims, 1 observable-only import limitation, 25 unreachable trusted primitives, and 832 declarations/rules outside the executed sublanguage. For the last group, their LHS/syntax cannot arise from this program's AST or intermediate configurations. Under the required witness standard there is no false conclusion they can enable on this intended path, so I do not label them unsound.
+
+### Used-syntax and control-flow map
+
+| Submitted construct | Declaration and operative supplied rules |
+|---|---|
+| `Module`, statement lists | `syntax.k`; `core.k` `#loadAll` and statement sequencing |
+| `ImportFrom("typing",...)` | `controls.k` non-math import no-op |
+| `FuncDef`, `Params` | `functions.k` closure binding |
+| `Call(Name("sum_product"),...)` | `core.k` lookup/argument evaluation; `call.k` closure call and frame allocation |
+| `Assign`, `Name`, `Int` | strict syntax; `core.k` literals/lookups; `controls.k` local assignment |
+| `For`, input list, loop target | proof-local For bridge; supplied `#loop/#loopStep/#loopLbl`; proof-local iterator bridge; supplied `#bindTgt` |
+| `BinOp("+")`, `BinOp("*")` | `operators.k` dispatch; `int.k` exact `+Int` and `*Int` cases |
+| `Return(TupleExpr(...))` | proof-local Return bridge; supplied `#pop`; equivalent supplied tuple/return rules |
+
+The `typing` import rule is deliberately broader than CPython because it does not bind `List`/`Tuple`. Those names are never read by the translated body, and the theorem intentionally leaves the final module-scope map existential. Thus the approximation cannot change the returned result or normal-control obligations and is acceptable for this observable theorem.
+
+### Proof-local extension inventory and decisions
+
+1. `IntList ::= .IntList | iList(Int,IntList)` is a constructor-complete representation of finite integer lists. It introduces no oracle or state.
+
+2. `intVals(.IntList) => .ValSeq` and `intVals(iList(I,R)) => vCons(I,intVals(R))` are disjoint, exhaustive, constructor-preserving, and structurally decreasing. They define the input embedding and do not skip program execution.
+
+3. `#iterNext(list(intVals(.IntList))) => #iterDone` is exactly the composition of the `intVals` base equation with supplied `MPY-LIST` iteration. The only affected cell is `<k>`.
+
+4. `#iterNext(list(intVals(iList(I,R)))) => #iterYield(I,list(intVals(R)))` is the corresponding exact cons composition. The empty/cons guards are constructor-disjoint and exhaustive. If the inner `intVals` equation fires before either priority rule, the supplied iterator rule reaches the same RHS, so the overlap agrees.
+
+5. The exact `For(Name("number"),Name("numbers"),BODY)` bridge applies only in a closed plain local frame where `numbers = list(intVals(XS))` and the other exact program locals exist. Fixed execution performs a pure local lookup, obtains that same list value, then uses `For(T,OBJ,B) => #loop(OBJ,T,B)`. The bridge reads `<env>/<scopes>`, changes only `<k>`, preserves every other cell, binding, body, and continuation, and has the same outcome. It does not execute or summarize the loop body.
+
+6. The exact `Return(TupleExpr(total,product))` bridge applies only in a closed plain local frame with integer accumulators and `noRet`. Fixed execution looks up `total` then `product` left-to-right, builds the two-element tuple, and applies supplied `Return(V) ~> _ => #pop` while setting `retV(V)`. The bridge reads `<env>/<scopes>`, writes exactly `<k>/<ret>`, and leaves supplied `#pop` to restore environment, remove the scope, restore `scopeLoc`, and continue. It preserves binding, evaluation order, control, and the full state footprint.
+
+7. `sumFrom` and `productFrom` are `[function,total]` only over `IntList`. Their base/cons equations are disjoint, exhaustive, and structurally decreasing. They are the literal left folds performed by the two real assignments. They constrain the result but never replace those assignments.
+
+There are no proof-local simplifications, opaque symbols, unchecked mathematical lemmas, result oracles, call interceptions, or rules that rewrite the loop body to an answer. All four priority bridges either compose pure fixed-semantics steps or agree with the path obtained by first reducing `intVals`.
+
+### Configuration, calls, state, and priorities
+
+The entry starts from the exact supplied configuration. The closure call allocates local scope 1, increments `scopeLoc`, pushes a frame containing the caller continuation/environment/saved location, and binds `numbers`. The loop uses the supplied target-binding and real assignments. The Return bridge does not perform cleanup; supplied `#pop` does. Entry postconditions constrain environment, `scopeLoc`, heap, heap counter, stack, return state, exception, and exit code. Only the semantically unobserved final scope map is existential.
+
+All used function cases have complete typed coverage, all relevant guarded overlaps are disjoint or agreeing, and integer operations use K mathematical `Int`, matching Python arbitrary-precision integer addition/multiplication on this domain. No allocation occurs for the bare claim input list or returned tuple, consistent with the supplied value representation; concrete Python-list allocations in the smoke driver affect only concrete heap evidence.
+
+### Opaque and unused trust boundaries
+
+The complete supplied opaque-symbol list is:
+
+```text
+sortVS, sortKeyVS,
+intFloatDiv, divII, floatMod, floatLt, absF, floorFI, toF, ceilF,
+subF, divF, addF, mulF, powF, gtF, eqF, decStrToF,
+divFloatIntV, intToF, truncF, roundF, roundFN, sqrtF,
+md5hexCodes
+```
+
+The submitted AST contains no floats, sorting, hashing, subscripting, strings, dictionaries, sets, or corresponding calls. None of these 25 symbols can occur on either proof path, and the proof module imports `MPY`, not the LLVM-only `MPY-CONCRETE` rules. They are genuine supplied trust boundaries but have no dependent claim here.
+
+I found no materially unsound reachable rule and therefore make no unsoundness allegation. Consistent with the required standard, no rule is called unsound without a concrete or symbolic false-conclusion witness. Stage 5 passes.
+
+## 6. Fresh non-vacuity test
+
+I did not reuse the candidate's `spec-vacuity.k`. The reviewer-authored [audit-vacuity.k](evidence/audit-vacuity.k) loads the exact submitted program, uses the satisfying concrete input `[1,2,3,4]`, retains the correct sum 10, and changes only the result-constraining product obligation from the demonstrably correct 24 to the false 25.
+
+First, a dry run parsed and compiled it successfully:
+
+```bash
+kprove audit-vacuity.k --definition audit-verification-kompiled \
+  --spec-module AUDIT-VACUITY --dry-run --warnings none
+# exit 0
+```
+
+See [06-mutation-dry-run.log](evidence/06-mutation-dry-run.log). The real proof command then exited 1 with `WarnStuckClaimState`. Its residual is a normal final configuration whose `<k>` cell contains `tuple(vCons(10,vCons(24,.ValSeq))) ~> .K`; it cannot unify with the mutated `(10,25)` destination. This is exactly the expected unmet result obligation, not a parse error, missing import, timeout, unrelated crash, or unreachable mutation. See [06-mutation-proof.log](evidence/06-mutation-proof.log).
+
+Stage 6 passes.
+
+## 7. Proven versus assumed accounting
+
+### Precisely what is machine-checked
+
+Under the supplied `MPY` rules plus the audited proof-local definitions/bridges, for every finite `IntList XS`, if execution of the exact submitted translated module/call terminates from the stated initial configuration, it reaches normal completion with return value:
+
+```k
+tuple(vCons(sumFrom(0, XS),
+            vCons(productFrom(1, XS), .ValSeq)))
+```
+
+The independently proved loop theorem establishes the analogous result from every matching loop-head state with arbitrary integer accumulators. This is partial correctness; it is not reported as a separate termination theorem.
+
+The proof also establishes normal `NoExc`/exit-code 0 completion, restored caller environment and scope counter, empty stack/return state, and unchanged heap/heap counter for the entry claim. It does not constrain the final module-scope map.
+
+### Trust and assumption ledger
+
+| Boundary | Dependents/effect | Assessment |
+|---|---|---|
+| Supplied `MPY` semantics tree | Defines syntax, configuration, execution, and all positive claims | Designated trusted baseline, integrity-verified and statically audited on every reachable rule. It is a Python subset, but covers every used construct. |
+| K v7.1.293 parser/compiler/Haskell/LLVM backends and builtin `Int`, Map/List, rewriting logic | All machine-checking and concrete execution | Ordinary unavoidable TCB; exact version/commands recorded. |
+| `SPEC.loop-invariant` passed through `--trusted` in the entry run | Entry claim only | Not a remaining assumption: the identical claim over the identical fresh definition was independently proved with exit 0 and `#Top` immediately beforehand. |
+| Proof-local For/Return/iterator bridges | Symbolic cooling/iteration points | No longer merely assumed: complete state footprints and exact fixed-step compositions are checked in Stage 5. Concrete end-to-end execution is supporting evidence only. |
+| `sumFrom`/`productFrom` meaning “sum/product” | Human-language intent bridge | Their constructor equations are exactly the ordinary finite left-fold definitions. The K theorem proves those values; identifying them with finite sum/product is elementary mathematics, supported but not replaced by differential tests. |
+| Trusted translator | Python source to submitted `.mpy` identity | Trusted input by audit authority; fresh regeneration is byte-identical. It supports source/program identity, not proof closure. |
+| Trusted canonical Python implementation and CPython | Natural-contract comparison | Finite empirical bridge only: 20,121 differential cases and concrete substitutions. It is not used as a K axiom or substitute for reachability. |
+| 25 supplied opaque float/sort/MD5 symbols | None | Unreachable; they affect neither value, control, state, nor termination assumptions of this theorem. |
+| Final scope-map existential | Module binding state only | Acceptable omission because HumanEval observes the returned tuple; all control/error/heap cells remain constrained. |
+
+Excluded behavior is non-integer elements, non-list/infinite iterables, Python resource exhaustion, and behavior outside the supplied subset. These exclusions agree with the stated finite `List[int]` contract and partial-correctness scope.
+
+### Final reasoning
+
+The dynamic gate is satisfied by fresh `#Top` results for every positive claim and the expected meaningful mutation failure. The static gate is satisfied because the actual submitted program is parser-identical to the claim, every proof-local extension is constructor-valid or an exact operational composition, no opaque or answer-smuggling symbol is reachable, and the result is fully constrained. The Python implementation agrees with the trusted canonical implementation across all documented/boundary cases and a substantial deterministic sample. No material adequacy or auditability gap remains.
+
+VERDICT: PASS
+LEGITIMACY: LEGIT
